Three relationships matter early:
Campaign → project. Keep the original campaign link and snapshot the brief, requirements, payout terms, and deadline at project creation. If the source later changes a deadline or payout, show the change and let the creator decide whether to update their working brief.
Asset ↔ project. A podcast recording can feed multiple clips. Each project can point to a different timestamp range in the same recording. Removing a project attachment leaves the shared asset available for other projects.
Project → drafts and submissions. A project can have several drafts and published clips. A submission can point to the exact version used, so a later revision leaves the submission history intact.
3. Main Workflows
Campaign Discovery and Daily Updates
Each source adapter should use a stable external listing ID when available. A failed check preserves the last successfully collected data and its timestamp. A listing’s disappearance should only change its availability when the integration gives enough evidence, such as an explicit closure or a complete listing feed with documented semantics. A pasted URL can always be saved with user-entered details, even when the source cannot provide a preview.
Upload, Edit, and Submit

For large footage, use direct multipart uploads. The API creates a pending asset and issues signed upload instructions. After upload, it verifies ownership and the stored object before making the asset available. External links retain their original provider’s permissions.
