Integrity and Visibility
Imported campaigns have a source and no owner. Manual campaigns have an owner and no source. A source listing is public within the signed-in campaign feed. Manual campaigns and everything downstream belong to their creator. Archived manual campaigns leave existing projects and history readable to their owner.
Imported campaign identity uses partial unique indexes on (source_id, external_id) where the external ID is present and on (source_id, canonical_url) where it is absent. When an adapter first learns an ID, reconcile an existing URL-only row before inserting. Manual campaigns can use a separate partial unique index on (owner_id, canonical_url) for active records with URLs.
Add UNIQUE(owner_id, id) to private parent tables and use composite foreign keys from private child rows. Use project-aware FKs for a project's final draft and for a submission's draft, so they always belong to the same project. Validate access to campaign rows before creating saves or projects. Reject cross-owner lookups with 404.
Deadline fields use deadline_date, deadline_at, and optional deadline_time_zone. A check permits at most one of date and instant. Preserve the source time zone when known. Display a date-only deadline as an all-day event and mark it overdue when the creator's local date passes it.
Campaign availability uses unknown | open | closed. Personal tracking uses saved | considering | in_progress | submitted | approved | rejected | completed. Submission status uses submitted | revision_requested | approved | rejected. The personal campaign state is a user-controlled summary across potentially several projects and submissions. Starting the first project advances a saved opportunity from saved or considering to in progress. Later submission changes leave that summary under the user's control.
Use a stable ID for every asset. Detaching an asset preserves its file and other attachments. Reject deletion with 409 while a segment, draft, or attachment references it. Persist a deletion job before deleting unreferenced storage objects. Archive projects with historical drafts/submissions.
Index common filters with leading owner and then status or deadline. Add indexes for source feed order, reverse join lookup by asset, submissions by project, payouts by receipt date, unread notifications, and every FK used to enforce deletes. Add PostgreSQL full-text search over campaign title and brief for the discovery feed, with the visibility filter in the same query.

2. REST API
Base path /api/v1. JSON fields use camelCase. Resource responses use { "data": ... }. Lists add nextCursor and accept a default limit of 25, maximum 100. Cursors bind to filters and use a stable sort with an ID tie-breaker. Calendar reads accept ISO dates from inclusive and to exclusive in the supplied IANA time zone. Use 201 for creation, 200 for reads and updates, 202 for accepted asynchronous imports, and 204 for deletion or logout.
Use secure, HttpOnly session cookies over HTTPS, check CSRF tokens for browser mutations, and rate-limit login, imports, and signed URL creation. Return errors as { "error": { "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR", "message": "...", "details": [], "requestId": "..." } }. Use 400 for invalid parameters or payloads, 401 for missing sessions, 404 for missing or inaccessible resources, 409 for invalid state transitions, stale edits or conflicting retries, and 429 for rate limits. Return a resource version for editable records and require If-Match on edits that could overwrite a brief, content plan, or draft selection.

