3. Transactions and Background Work
Start Project. Authenticate, validate campaign visibility, lock or insert the idempotency record, create or update the save, capture the campaign snapshot, create the project, and store the response in one transaction. A repeat key and identical body replay the stored success. A changed body returns 409. A new key permits an intentional second project. Retain keys for at least 24 hours and document the retry window.
Upload. First persist a pending asset and upload session. Initialize provider multipart state, then return signed part URLs in batches as requested. Validate declared size and configured quotas before signing. Lock or claim the completion state, verify storage size and upload identity, and mark the asset ready atomically with the session update. If storage completion succeeds before the database commit, a retry can verify the existing object under the same generated key and finish the transition. Cleanup expires abandoned uploads and aborts incomplete multipart sessions. Configure bucket lifecycle cleanup as a fallback. An external link keeps its provider permissions.
Source checks. Schedule daily runs for enabled, permitted adapters. Upsert stable identities and record changes transactionally with the successful run. Preserve previous records and freshness on a failed check. Infer closure from absence only when a complete feed has documented closure semantics. If an import adapter is unavailable, keep the editable manual listing and return its enrichment status. Accept only validated provider URLs for server-side enrichment and reject redirects outside approved provider hosts.
Reminders. Generate from current deadlines and re-check the latest value before delivery. Deduplicate per recipient, entity, deadline value and reminder offset. Write notifications with a unique key so retries do not create duplicates. Use the same deduplication principle for source change notifications.

